  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 review is already showing early scholarly uptake: OpenAlex records 1 citation, and the article has also been reused in 2 downstream contexts, including a 2026 preprint in bioRxiv. The record suggests the paper is beginning to circulate within the growing in vivo Perturb-seq literature on neuropsychiatric disease mechanisms.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Shi and Jin\u2019s article frames an important problem in psychiatric genetics: many risk alleles are known for disorders such as schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, and major depressive disorder, but their mechanistic links to circuit-level pathology remain difficult to resolve. The review highlights in vivo pooled CRISPR screening with single-cell readout as a way to connect genetic perturbation to cell-state changes directly in animal tissue, offering a functional route from association to mechanism.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article\u2019s impact profile is still early, but it is not empty. One citation has been recorded, and the work appears in Europe PMC as a biomedical article mention. In addition, downstream reuse has been documented in a preprint focused on genome-scale functional mapping of the mammalian whole brain with in vivo Perturb-seq, indicating that the paper is being used as part of the methodological and conceptual foundation for follow-on work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The funding record also shows substantial support from agencies and foundations with strong interests in neuroscience, genetics, and translational biology, including the NIMH and NHGRI, alongside the Chan Zuckerberg Initiative, the California Institute for Regenerative Medicine, Pew Charitable Trusts, and the Conrad Prebys Foundation. That mix fits the article\u2019s position at the intersection of genomic technology development and neuropsychiatric disease research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record points to a review that is beginning to influence an emerging research area rather than one with mature citation accumulation. The available evidence emphasizes scholarly reuse over broad public attention: there are no documented event-data mentions, and the current footprint is concentrated in technical, research-facing venues. For a recent article in a fast-moving methods space, that is a credible early sign of relevance.</p>",
